**(i) Metamorphic facies (2 marks)**

A **metamorphic facies** is a **group (set) of metamorphic mineral assemblages that formed under a particular range of pressure and temperature conditions, in equilibrium**, so that rocks of different chemical composition give mineral assemblages that are related to the same conditions. The idea (Eskola, 1915) is that **a given facies is recognised by the mineral assemblage found in rocks of a given composition**, normally basic (mafic) rocks, and each facies is named after the characteristic minerals of such a rock (greenschist, amphibolite, granulite, blueschist, eclogite, hornfels). The facies is a way of showing the **grade** (intensity) of metamorphism and the P-T setting.

**(ii) Facies of contact and of regional metamorphism (6 marks)**

**Contact (thermal) metamorphism** occurs around igneous intrusions, at **low pressure and high temperature**, with no directed stress; rocks are **non-foliated and fine to medium grained (hornfels)**; the facies are:

- **Albite-epidote hornfels facies**: the lowest temperature (about 300 to 500 °C), found at the outer edge of an aureole;
- **Hornblende hornfels facies**: moderate temperature (about 500 to 650 °C); hornblende and plagioclase with cordierite, andalusite in pelites;
- **Pyroxene hornfels facies**: high temperature (about 650 to 800 °C or more), near the contact; pyroxene, cordierite, sillimanite; hornblende is lost;
- **Sanidinite facies**: very high temperature and very low pressure, in contact with hot volcanic bodies or xenoliths (glassy and partly melted rocks).

**Regional metamorphism** occurs over large areas in **orogenic (mountain) belts** under **both directed pressure and high temperature**, giving **foliated rocks** (slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss). The facies, from **low to high grade**, are:

- **Zeolite and prehnite-pumpellyite facies**: very low grade (about 100 to 300 °C), the start of metamorphism, lowest pressure;
- **Greenschist facies**: low grade (about 300 to 500 °C), minerals chlorite, albite, epidote, muscovite, actinolite;
- **Epidote-amphibolite and amphibolite facies**: medium to high grade (about 500 to 700 °C), hornblende and plagioclase, with garnet, staurolite, kyanite and sillimanite in pelites;
- **Granulite facies**: the highest temperature (above about 700 °C), pyroxene and plagioclase, dry rocks, may begin to melt (migmatites);
- **Blueschist facies**: **high pressure, low temperature** (glaucophane, lawsonite), formed in subduction zones;
- **Eclogite facies**: **very high pressure**, with garnet and omphacite (sodium-rich pyroxene), in deep subduction or the lower crust.

**Difference in brief:** contact facies have low pressure and a wide range of temperature, and the rocks are non-foliated; regional facies depend on both pressure and temperature (a Barrovian series: chlorite, biotite, garnet, staurolite, kyanite, sillimanite zones) and produce foliated rocks.
